"Internal Error ()\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/EfficientPPML/MORPH/commits/main/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/EfficientPPML/MORPH/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/161.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBump rust crates' version · ingonyama-zk/icicle@6c77775 · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/commit/6c77775)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/commit/6c77775\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 333\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity26-lu-tao.pdf)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 20; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view4\",\"pattern\":\"witness\"}); Total lines: 1992\nL130@P2: discloses nothing about the secret witness; and (3) Succinct\u0002ness, implying the proof size is small, typically logarithmic\nL131@P2: or constant to the circuit size, and verification is significantly\nL132@P2: faster than native computation. Structurally, zk-SNARKs are\nL133@P2: typically constructed using a modular framework that com\u0002poses a Polynomial Interactive Oracle Proof (PIOP) [4] with\nL134@P2: a Polynomial Commitment Scheme (PCS) [27].\nL135@P2-3: 5850 35th USENIX Security Symposium USENIX AssociationIn the domain of PIOPs, Plonk [13] and Marlin [8] serve\nL136@P3: as representative protocols that utilize univariate polynomials.\nL137@P3: However, they rely heavily on Fast Fourier Transforms (FFTs)\nL138@P3: for polynomial arithmetic, which incur O(nlogn) complex\u0002ity. To address this bottleneck, recent protocols like Spar\u0002tan [47] and HyperPlonk [6] have shifted towards multilinear\nL208@P3: basis with k coefficients in Fp. The notation s ← Fp\nL209@P3: k denotes\nL210@P3: sampling s uniformly at random from Fp\nL211@P3: k .\nL212@P3: 2.2 Zero-Knowledge Proof\nL213@P3: Zero-Knowledge Proof (ZKP) [17] is a cryptographic prim\u0002itive that enables a prover P to convince a verifier V of the\nL214@P3: validity of a statement x without revealing the underlying se\u0002cret witness w. As discussed in Section 1.2, modern ZKPs\nL215@P3: are typically constructed by PIOPs and PCSs. In this paper,\nL216@P3: we target sum-check-based PIOPs and linear-time coding\u0002based PCSs, utilizing HyperPlonk [6] and Brakedown [18] as\nL217@P3: our case studies. This architectural choice notably eliminates\nL218@P3: the need for computationally expensive FFTs and MSMs,\nL219@P3: enabling strictly linear O(n) proof generation complexity.\nL220@P3-4: USENIX Association 35th USENIX Security Symposium 58512.2.1 HyperPlonk\nL221@P4: We utilize HyperPlonk [6] as the PIOP to map circuit satisfia\u0002bility into polynomial relations. Figure 1 outlines an overview\nL222@P4: of HyperPlonk, which decomposes circuits into two cate\u0002gories: (1) gate identities and (2) wiring identities.\nL223@P4: The gate identities enforce arithmetic correctness by unify\u0002ing addition and multiplication logic into a single polynomial\nL224@P4: constraint. For a circuit of size N = 2\nL225@P4: µ\nL226@P4: , we associate each\nL227@P4: gate with a unique coordinate x on the Boolean hypercube\nL228@P4: {0,1}\nL229@P4: µ\nL230@P4: . Let w{a,b,c}(x) denote the polynomials for left, right,\nL231@P4: and output wires, and q{A,M,O}(x) be the selector polynomials\nL232@P4: acting as structural switches. The system defines gate types\nL233@P4: by setting qA(x) = qO(x) = 1,qM(x) = 0 for the addition gate\nL234@P4: and qM(x) = qO(x) = 1,qA(x) = 0 for the multiplication gate.\nL235@P4: The circuit validity is encoded as ensuring that Pgate(x) = 0\nL236@P4: for all x ∈ {0,1}\nL237@P4: µ\nL238@P4: , where Pgate(x) defined as:\nL239@P4: qA(x)(wa(x) +wb(x)) +qM(x)wa(x)wb(x)−qO(x)wc(x)\nL240@P4: (1)\nL241@P4: This zero-check condition can be reduced to a sum-check\nL242@P4: instance using Schwartz-Zippel Lemma [45]:\nL243@P4: ∑\nL244@P4: x∈{0,1}\nL245@P4: µ\nL246@P4: Pgate(x)· eq(x, r) = 0, (2)\nL247@P4: where r ∈ (Fp\nL248@P4: k )\nL249@P4: µ\nL250@P4: are random challenges from verifier, and\nL251@P4: eq(x,r) is defined as the product of linear terms:\nL252@P4: eq(x,r) =\nL253@P4: µ−1\nL254@P4: ∏\nL255@P4: i=0\nL256@P4: ((1−xi)(1−ri) +xiri). (3)\nL257@P4: The wiring identities enforce global consistency by ensur\u0002ing that connected wires across different gates. To verify this,\nL258@P4: we aggregate the individual wire polynomials w{a,b,c}into a\nL259@P4: single unified witness w by introducing two auxiliary vari\u0002ables y ∈ {0,1}\nL260@P4: 2\nL261@P4: . We define polynomial w over the extended\nL262@P4: hypercube {0,1}\nL263@P4: 2 × {0,1}µ\nL264@P4: as:\nL265@P4: w(0,0,x) = wa(x),w(0,1,x) = wb(x),w(1,0,x) = wc(x),\nL266@P4: (4)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity26-lu-tao.pdf)\nciteturn4view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 20; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view4\",\"pattern\":\"Goldilocks\"}); Total lines: 1992\nNo matching text found for \"Goldilocks\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity26-lu-tao.pdf)\nciteturn4view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 20; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view4\",\"pattern\":\"field\"}); Total lines: 1992\nL73@P2: low-precision arithmetic (e.g., FP8, UINT8), rendering them\nL74@P2: ill-suited for the finite field arithmetic required by ZKPs.\nL75@P2: Traditional element-wise implementations decompose high\u0002precision field elements into multiple limbs, processing them\nL76@P2: via independent scalar operations. This method incurs signifi\u0002cant overhead due to fine-grained carry propagation and mod\u0002ular reduction, undermining the hardware’s acceleration po\u0002tential. Challenge #2: Structural Divergence. Tensor Cores\nL77@P2: are fixed-function units designed exclusively for matrix mul\u0002tiplication. In contrast, core ZKP primitives, such as sum\u0002check protocols and linear-time encoders, do not naturally\nL78@P2: align with this matrix paradigm. Effectively bridging this gap\nL79@P2: requires a hardware-algorithm co-design approach to refor\u0002mulate these operations, ensuring compatibility with Tensor\nL80@P2: Cores. Challenge #3: System-Level Constraints. Unlike\nL81@P2: general-purpose SIMT cores that support flexible memory ac\u0002cess and thread execution, Tensor Cores mandate highly struc\u0002tured data layouts with fixed tile sizes, alongside a strict coop\u0002erative execution model where threads within a warp must op\u0002erate synchronously. These architectural discrepancies render\nL82@P2: state-of-the-art SIMT-based frameworks [7, 24, 26, 34, 35, 37]\nL83@P2: functionally incompatible with Tensor Core architectures.\nL84@P2: 1.1 Contributions\nL85@P2: We introduce TensorZKP, the first GPU framework leveraging\nL86@P2: Tensor Cores for ZKP acceleration, which addresses the afore\u0002mentioned challenges through the following contributions:\nL87@P2: • Matrix-Centric Framework for Field Arithmetic. We\nL88@P2: design a novel framework that reformulates finite field arith\u0002metic into a matrix-centric paradigm. Specifically, depart\u0002ing from the traditional approach that treats field arithmetic\nL89@P2: as a sequence of element-wise scalar operations, we de\u0002velop specialized formulations for two fundamental prim\u0002itives over fields: vector inner products and vector-scalar\nL90@P2: multiplication. By aggregating the decomposed 8-bit limbs\nL91@P2: of entire input vectors into unified blocks, we transform\nL92@P2: two primitives into matrix multiplication tasks. This for\u0002mulation not only maps field arithmetic into low-precision\nL93@P2: matrix operations but also leverages this collective structure\nL94@P2: to enable lazy modular reduction, significantly amortizing\nL95@P2: the cost of expensive modular arithmetic.\nL96@P2: • Algorithm-Hardware Co-design of ZKP Modules. Be\u0002yond low-level arithmetic, we fundamentally redesign com\u0002plex ZKP modules, including sum-check protocols [36]\nL97@P2: and linear-time encoders [48], to strictly align with both\nL98@P2: the compute capability and memory hierarchy of Tensor\nL99@P2: Cores. We restructure their dataflow into dense matrix mul\u0002tiplication tasks, enforcing tiled memory access patterns\nL100@P2: that maximize L2 cache locality on GPUs. Furthermore,\nL101@P2: we instantiate these optimizations within HyperPlonk [6]\nL102@P2: (PIOP) and Brakedown [18] (PCS) to enable end-to-end\nL103@P2: proof generation. To the best of our knowledge, this is the\nL104@P2: first work to map ZKP workloads on Tensor Core matrix\nL105@P2: units, following the dominant trend of AI-driven hardware\nL106@P2: evolution.\nL107@P2: • Asynchronous Warp-Specialized Execution. We architect\nL108@P2: an asynchronous execution model using warp specializa\u0002tion to maximize heterogeneous GPU utilization. First, we\nL109@P2: leverage a producer-consumer model to decouple memory\nL110@P2: access and on-chip computation using different execution\nL111@P2: warps asynchronously, to effectively masks global memory\nL112@P2: latency and arithmetic overheads. Furthermore, we manages\nL113@P2: the fine-grained execution between Tensor-Core-driven ma\u0002trix accumulation and SIMT-based field modular reduction.\nL189@P3: where xi denotes the i-th element of x. We denote the stan\u0002dard inner product of two vectors as ⟨x,y⟩ = ∑xiyi\nL190@P3: , and the\nL191@P3: Hadamard product as x ◦ y. Similarly, we use bold upper-case\nL192@P3: letters, such as M, to represent matrices, where Mi, j denotes\nL193@P3: the element in the i-th row and j-th column. We denote the\nL194@P3: matrix multiplication of two matrices A and B as A×B.\nL195@P3: We let Fp denote a prime field of order p and Fp\nL196@P3: k de\u0002note an extension field. Let λ be the security parameter.\nL197@P3: To ensure λ-bit security, we require the field size to sat\u0002isfy log2\nL198@P3: (p\nL199@P3: k\nL200@P3: ) ≥ λ, where typically λ ∈ {128,256}. To align\nL201@P3: with low-precision hardware, we use a t-bit limb decompo\u0002sition. We define a decomposition function limb(·,w) that\nL202@P3: maps an element a ∈ Fp to a vector of limbs a ∈ [0,2\nL203@P3: w)l with\nL204@P3: a = limb(a). This decomposition satisfies a = ⟨a,b⟩, where\nL205@P3: b = (1,2\nL206@P3: w,22w,...,2(l−1)w) is the basis vector. For the ex\u0002tension field Fp\nL207@P3: k , we represent its element via a polynomial\nL449@P6: cryptographic workloads to this specialized hardware necessi\u0002tates converting algebraic operations into matrix-compatible\nL450@P6: formats without incurring prohibitive overhead. We address\nL451@P6: this challenge through a bottom-up design methodology, sys\u0002tematically progressing from matrix-centric field arithmetic\nL452@P6: to Tensor-Core-compatible ZKP modules. In addition, we\nL453@P6: architect an asynchronous execution model using warp spe\u0002cialization to maximize heterogeneous GPU utilization.\nL454@P6: First, matrix-centric finite field arithmetic. The foun\u0002dation of TensorZKP is a data representation that bridges\nL455@P6: the granularity gap between cryptographic requirements and\nL456@P6: hardware constraints. Standard finite field arithmetic operates\nL457@P6: on elements in Fp\nL458@P6: k , which are incompatible with the UINT8-\nL459@P6: based Tensor Cores. A naive adaptation is to encode each field\nL460@P6: element as multiple UINT8 limbs for scalar operations, such\nL461@P6: as field multiplication a · b ∈ Fp\nL462@P6: k . However, this approach is\nL463@P6: inefficient: it introduces fine-grained carry-propagation de\u0002pendencies and a great amount of moduler reduction.\nL464@P6: To resolve this, we shift the optimization scope from in\u0002dividual scalar instructions to field vectors, reformulating al\u0002gebraic operations into uniform matrix workloads. First, we\nL465@P6: propose a limb-based vector-to-matrix layout transformation.\nL466@P6: Formally, we reshape a cryptographic input vector v ∈ (Fp\nL467@P6: k )\nL468@P6: N\nL469@P6: into a dense matrix V ∈ UINT8N×kL by decomposing each\nL470@P6: field element into kL limbs and stacking them as rows, where\nL471@P6: L denotes the number of limbs required to represent the prime\nL472@P6: p. Building on this layout, we map vector primitives to matrix\nL473@P6: instructions. For the vector inner product, we transform the\nL474@P6: operation z = ⟨v,u⟩ into a matrix multiplication Z = V\nL475@P6: ⊤ ×U.\nL476@P6: This produces a compact kL×kL result matrix. In addition,\nL477@P6: we defer the expensive modulo reduction M (·) until after\nL478@P6: the full matrix aggregation, computing z = M (Z) only once.\nL479@P6: Similarly, for scalar-vector multiplication z = c · v, we note\nL480@P6: that field multiplication can be viewed as linear convolution\nL481@P6: operations. Therefore, we transform the scalar c ∈ Fp\nL482@P6: k into\nL483@P6: a structured convolution matrix C and compute Z = V×C.\nL484@P6: This redesign converts vector arithmetic into matrix opera\u0002tions. We provide further details in Section 4.\nL485@P6: Second, Tensor-Core-Compatible ZKP Modules. Al\u0002though matrix-centric arithmetic establishes the foundational\nL565@P7: for modular reduction while Tensor Cores execute the matrix\nL566@P7: multiplications. We provide further details in Section 6.\nL567@P7: 4 Matrix-Centric Finite Field Arithmetic\nL568@P7: ZKPs rely heavily on arithmetic over finite fields, typically\nL569@P7: prime fields Fp or extension fields Fp\nL570@P7: k . To ensure λ-bit secu\u0002rity, we require the field size to satisfy log2\nL571@P7: (p\nL572@P7: k\nL573@P7: ) ≥ λ, where\nL574@P7: typically λ ∈ {128,256}. However, such field arithmetic is\nL575@P7: fundamentally incompatible with Tensor Cores, which sup\u0002port only low-precision data formats such as UINT8.\nL576@P7: To overcome this, we shift the optimization target from\nL577@P7: individual scalar instructions to vector-level operations:\nL578@P7: (i) The first operation is the vector inner product, where\nL579@P7: v = (v0,v1,...,vN−1),u = (u0,u1,...,uN−1),vi,ui ∈ Fp\nL580@P7: k ,\nL581@P7: and the computation is\nL582@P7: z = ⟨v,u⟩ =\nL583@P7: N−1\nL584@P7: ∑\nL585@P7: i=0\nL586@P7: viui.\nL587@P7: (ii) The second operation is vector-scalar multiplication ,\nL588@P7: where a vector\nL589@P7: v = [v0,v1,...,vN−1], vi ∈ Fp\nL590@P7: k\nL591@P7: is multiplied by a field scalar c ∈ Fp\nL592@P7: k , resulting in\nL593@P7: z = c · v = [c · v0,c · v1,..., c · vN−1].\nL594@P7: We reformulate two vector primitives to enable their accel\u0002eration on Tensor Cores. Building on this foundation, Section\nL595@P7: 5 further details how we reconstruct higher-level ZKP mod\u0002ules, including sum-check protocols and Spielman codes, in\nL596@P7: terms of these two vector operations. Moreover, because these\nL597@P7: primitives capture fundamental arithmetic patterns over finite\nL598@P7: fields, the same reformulation strategy can be extended to\nL599@P7: accelerate other cryptographic algorithms on Tensor Cores.\nL600@P7: 4.1 Matrix Construction for Finite Fields\nL601@P7: To enable vector arithmetic on Tensor Cores, we must bridge\nL602@P7: the layout gap by mapping both field scalars and vectors\nL603@P7: into UINT8 matrix structures. This layout transformation is\nL604@P7: essential to adapt the cryptographic operands to the native\nL605@P7: input requirements of the Tensor Core units.\nL606@P7: Encode field elements as multiple UINT8 limbs. For ex\u0002tension fields Fp\nL607@P7: k utilized in ZKP protocols, the cryptographic\nL608@P7: security level is related to the bit-length of the field size p\nL609@P7: k\nL610@P7: .\nL611@P7: Without loss of generality, we use a configuration where p\nL612@P7: is a 32-bit prime and k = 4. This yields a 128-bit extension\nL613@P7: field Fp\nL614@P7: 4 , offering sufficient security strength for modern pro\u0002tocols. Consequently, we represent each element a ∈ Fp\nL615@P7: 4 as a\nL616@P7: degree-3 polynomial over the base field Fp:\nL617@P7: a = a\nL618@P7: ⟨0⟩ +a⟨1⟩X +a⟨2⟩X2 +a⟨3⟩X3\nL619@P7: , where a\nL620@P7: ⟨s⟩ ∈ Fp, (9)\nL621@P7: and X denotes the root of the irreducible polynomial P(X).\nL622@P7: However, the base field coefficients a\nL623@P7: ⟨s⟩\nL624@P7: are 32-bit integers,\nL625@P7: which exceed the native UINT8 precision of Tensor Cores. To\nL626@P7: bridge this gap, we further decompose each 32-bit coefficient\nL627@P7: into a sequence of 8-bit limbs. Let L denote the number of\nL628@P7: limbs required to represent the prime p (for a 32-bit prime,\nL629@P7: L = 4). We express each coefficient a\nL630@P7: ⟨s⟩\nL631@P7: , for s ∈ {0,1,2,3},\nL632@P7: using a radix-28representation:\nL633@P7: a\nL634@P7: ⟨s⟩ =\nL635@P7: L−1\nL636@P7: ∑\nL637@P7: j=0\nL638@P7: a\nL639@P7: ⟨s⟩\nL640@P7: j\nL641@P7: · 2\nL642@P7: 8 j\nL643@P7: , with a\nL644@P7: ⟨s⟩\nL645@P7: j ∈ [0,2\nL646@P7: 8 −1]. (10)\nL647@P7: Through this encoding, a single high-precision element a ∈\nL648@P7: Fp\nL649@P7: 4 is mapped to 4L UINT8 values, denoted as:\nL650@P7: [a\nL651@P7: ⟨0⟩\nL652@P7: 0\nL653@P7: ,...,a\nL654@P7: ⟨0⟩\nL655@P7: L−1\nL656@P7: ,...,a\nL657@P7: ⟨3⟩\nL658@P7: 0\nL659@P7: ,...,a\nL660@P7: ⟨3⟩\nL661@P7: L−1\nL662@P7: ].\nL663@P7: Vector-to-Matrix Layout Transformation. Consider a\nL664@P7: vector a ∈ (Fp\nL665@P7: k )\nL666@P7: N consisting of N elements, denoted as\nL667@P7: a = [a0,a1,...,aN−1]. To fully exploit matrix arithmetic used\nL668@P7: in Tensor Cores, we transform this one-dimensional vector\nL669@P7: structure into a two-dimensional matrix layout suitable for\nL670@P7: hardware matrix units.\nL671@P7: We construct a matrix A ∈ UINT8N×kL by stacking the limb\u0002decomposed representation of each element ai as a distinct\nL672@P7: row. Recall that each element ai ∈ Fp\nL673@P7: k consists of k coeffi\u0002cients, and each coefficient is decomposed into L limbs. Con\u0002sequently, the matrix A is defined such that the i-th row cor\u0002responds to the i-th element of the vector a, and the columns\nL674@P7: store the flattened sequence of limbs. Formally, each entry of\nL675@P7: the matrix A is given by:\nL676@P7: Ai,(sL+j) = (ai)\nL677@P7: ⟨s⟩\nL678@P7: j\nL679@P7: , (11)\nL680@P7: where i ∈ [0,N −1] denotes the element index, s ∈ [0, k −1]\nL681@P7: denotes the coefficient degree, and j ∈ [0,L−1] denotes the\nL682@P7: limb significance.\nL683@P7: Visually, this transformation reshapes the abstract vector\nL684@P7-8: USENIX Association 35th USENIX Security Symposium 5855into a dense integer matrix:\nL685@P8: A =\nL686@P8: \nL687@P8: \nL688@P8: \nL689@P8: \nL690@P8: \nL691@P8: limbs of a0\nL692@P8: limbs of a1\nL693@P8: .\nL694@P8: .\nL695@P8: .\nL696@P8: limbs of aN−1\nL697@P8: \nL698@P8: \nL699@P8: \nL700@P8: \nL701@P8: \nL702@P8: N×kL\nL703@P8: (12)\nL704@P8: For our specific configuration with k = 4 and L = 4, each field\nL705@P8: element occupies one row of 16 UINT8 columns. This layout\nL706@P8: aligns with the dimension requirements of Tensor Cores.\nL707@P8: Scalar-to-Matrix Layout Transformation. In addition to\nL708@P8: vector operands, field scalars c ∈ Fp\nL709@P8: k must also be mapped to\nL710@P8: the matrix domain to leverage Tensor Cores. Recall that our\nL711@P8: elements are encoded as sequences of limbs. Mathematically,\nL712@P8: the multiplication of two multi-limb integers is equivalent to\nL713@P8: the discrete linear convolution of their limb sequences. To\nL714@P8: perform this convolution using hardware matrix multipliers,\nL715@P8: we transform each constituent base-field coefficient c\nL716@P8: ⟨s⟩ ∈ Fp\nL717@P8: of the scalar c into a structured matrix.\nL718@P8: Specifically, let the coefficient c\nL719@P8: ⟨s⟩ be decomposed into L\nL720@P8: limbs: [c\nL721@P8: ⟨s⟩\nL722@P8: 0\nL723@P8: ,...,c\nL724@P8: ⟨s⟩\nL725@P8: L−1\nL726@P8: ]. To compute the product of each row in\nL727@P8: the data matrix with this scalar, we construct a scalar matrix\nL728@P8: C\nL729@P8: ⟨s⟩ ∈ UINT8L×2L\nL730@P8: . The matrix C\nL731@P8: ⟨s⟩\nL732@P8: encapsulates the shift-and\u0002add logic of convolution by arranging the limbs. Formally,\nL733@P8: the entry at row u and column v is defined as:\nL734@P8: C\nL735@P8: ⟨s⟩\nL736@P8: u,v =\nL737@P8: (\nL738@P8: c\nL739@P8: ⟨s⟩\nL740@P8: v−u\nL741@P8: if 0 ≤ v−u < L,\nL742@P8: 0 otherwise.\nL743@P8: (13)\nL744@P8: With the base-field scalar matrix C\nL745@P8: ⟨s⟩ defined, we now ex\u0002tend this construction to support the full arithmetic of the ex\u0002tension field Fp\nL746@P8: k . Multiplication in Fpk involves multiplying\nL747@P8: two polynomials: a(X) = ∑a\nL748@P8: ⟨i⟩Xi\nL749@P8: and c(X) = ∑c\nL750@P8: ⟨i⟩Xi\nL751@P8: . The\nL752@P8: coefficients of the product d(X) = a(X)·c(X) are obtained via\nL753@P8: the convolution of the coefficient sequences of a and c. Since\nL754@P8: our data matrix A stores the coefficients [a\nL755@P8: ⟨0⟩\nL756@P8: ,...,a\nL757@P8: ⟨k−1⟩\nL758@P8: ] hor\u0002izontally, we can compute the polynomial product d(X) by\nL759@P8: right-multiplying A with a extension scalar matrix C.\nL760@P8: For our specific configuration with k = 4, the scalar c con\u0002sists of coefficients c\nL761@P8: ⟨0⟩\nL762@P8: ,..., c\nL763@P8: ⟨3⟩\nL764@P8: . We construct the global ma\u0002trix C by arranging the sub-matrices C\nL765@P8: ⟨s⟩\nL766@P8: as follows:\nL767@P8: \nL768@P8: \nL769@P8: \nL847@P8: vector. Consequently, each element in Z serves by compress\u0002ing each integer sub-block into a scalar base field element\nL848@P8: z\nL849@P8: (s,t) ∈ Fp:\nL850@P8: z\nL851@P8: (s,t) =\nL852@P8: \nL853@P8: L−1\nL854@P8: ∑\nL855@P8: i=0\nL856@P8: L−1\nL857@P8: ∑\nL858@P8: j=0\nL859@P8: Z\nL860@P8: (s,t)\nL861@P8: i, j\nL862@P8: · 2\nL863@P8: 8(i+j)\nL864@P8: !\nL865@P8: (mod p). (17)\nL866@P8: This operation maps the dense integer matrix into k\nL867@P8: 2 discrete\nL868@P8: coefficients. Since the term associated with the pair (s,t) con\u0002tributes to the polynomial degree s +t, the extension field\nL869@P8: element z can be aggregated by:\nL870@P8: z =\nL871@P8: k−1\nL872@P8: ∑\nL873@P8: s=0\nL874@P8: k−1\nL875@P8: ∑\nL876@P8: t=0\nL877@P8: z\nL878@P8: (s,t)\nL879@P8: ·X\nL880@P8: s+t\nL889@P8: Vector-scalar multiplication. The multiplication of a\nL890@P8: vector v ∈ (Fp\nL891@P8: k )\nL892@P8: N by a field scalar c ∈ Fp\nL893@P8: k is defined as\nL894@P8: z = c · v = [c · v0,c · v1,...,c · vN−1]. We reformulate this op\u0002eration as a matrix multiplication. Let V ∈ UINT8N×kL be the\nL895@P8: matrix representation of vector v, and let C ∈ UINT8kL×4kL be\nL896@P8: the structured matrix derived from the scalar c, constructed\nL897@P8: via the Scalar-to-Matrix Layout Transformation. We compute\nL898@P8: the operation by executing:\nL899@P8: Z = V×C, (19)\nL1352@P12: Setup. We implement TensorZKP in the C++/CUDA lan\u0002guage and compile it with nvcc 12.6. All GPU experiments\nL1353@P12: are conducted on an NVIDIA H100 equipped with 80 GB\nL1354@P12: of high-bandwidth memory (HBM). Although our primary\nL1355@P12: benchmarks target the H100 architecture, the implementation\nL1356@P12: is compatible with Tensor-Core–enabled GPUs, such as the\nL1357@P12: NVIDIA A100. CPU baselines are run on Xeon® Platinum\nL1358@P12: 8480+. We utilize the BabyBear prime field extended to de\u0002gree k = 8 as our underlying finite field. This configuration\nL1381@P12: proof size and verifier time are identical to those of the stan\u0002dard CPU-based HyperPlonk+Brakedown implementation.\nL1382@P12: 7.2 Evaluating Field Arithmetic\nL1383@P12: We initiate our evaluation by micro-benchmarking the two\nL1384@P12: core primitives designed in Section 4: the Tensor-Compatible\nL1385@P12: Vector Inner Product and Vector-Scalar Multiplication. All\nL1386@P12: experiments utilize a consistent finite field implementation,\nL1387@P12: with input vector sizes N ranging from 2\nL1388@P12: 20 to 228. We bench\u00022\nL1389@P12: 20 222 224 226 228\nL1390@P12: Vector size\nL1391@P12: 10\nL1392@P12: −1\nL1393@P12: 10\nL1394@P12: 1\nL1395@P12: 10\n"
